    <exercise>
        <introduction>
            <p>
                Let <m>f(x)</m> be the function that gives the fine for a 
                speeding ticket as a function of the number of miles per 
                hour you go over the speed limit <m>x</m>. 
                According to <url href="https://www.myimprov.com/defensive-driving/michigan/how-much-is-a-speeding-ticket-in-michigan/">
                this (perhaps unreliable) website</url> the speeding fines are 
                defined by the following piecewise function of the miles per 
                hour over the speed limit:
	<md> f(x) = \begin{cases} 
	125 \amp 1\le x \le 5 \\
	135 \amp 5\lt x\leq 10 \\
	140 \amp 10\lt x\leq 15\\
	150 \amp 15\lt x\leq 20\\
	160 \amp20\lt x \leq 25\\
	170  \amp 25\lt x \leq 30\\
	180  \amp 30\lt x \leq 35\\
	190 \amp x \gt 35
\end{cases}
</md>
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task workspace="0.5in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                   What is the meaning of <m>f(3) = 125</m>? 
                   Include units in your explanation.
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.5in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    If the fine is  $140 what can you conclude about the speed of the driver?
                    </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.5in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Is the inverse of <m>f</m> a function? Why or why not? (Explain.)
                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Suppose the police decided to give a 5 mile per hour "buffer". 
                    That is, if you were going, say, 17 miles per hour over the speed 
                    limit, they would write the ticket for 12 mph over.
                     Which of the following function transformations would this be 
                     represented by? Explain your answer.
               
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>f(x) + 5</m>
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>f(x) - 5</m>
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>f(x + 5)</m>
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>f(x - 5)</m>
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>5f(x)</m>
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <m>\frac15f(x)</m>
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>   
             </p>
            </statement>
            
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.5in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Since speeding in a school zone is considered more 
                    dangerous, the fines increase by 20%. 
                    How could you represent this with a function 
                    transformation? Explain.

            </p>
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.5in">
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Suppose that it costs an additional $100 if you are given a 
                    ticket for speeding and also have an expired registration. 
                    How could you represent this with a function transformation?
                           </p>

            
            </statement>
        </task>

        <task workspace="0.05in">
            <statement>
                <p>
Your European friend wants to know how this translates to their homeland. 
One kilometer  is equivalent to 0.621371 miles. So <m>{m(x) = 0.621371x}</m> 
is a function that gives miles per hours based on <m>x</m> kilometers 
per hour. How can you combine <m>m(x)</m> and <m>f(x)</m> 
to give the speeding fine as a function of kilometers per hour?                </p>
            </statement>
        </task>


    </exercise>
</worksheet>